Found in read-only bug hunt (langertha-reviewer, 2026-09-25). Confirmed by code reading (no Langfuse endpoint contacted).

Where:
- lib/Langertha/Plugin/Langfuse.pm:362 and lib/Langertha/Role/Langfuse.pm:496: LWP::UserAgent->new(agent => ...) with no timeout -> LWP default 180s. Synchronous $ua->request.
- Plugin::Langfuse plugin_after_llm_response / after_image_gen / after_embedding call $self->flush when auto_flush (Plugin/Langfuse.pm:476, 537, 581). These are async hooks awaited inside Chat::simple_chat_f / chat_with_tools_f; a sync LWP call there blocks the whole IO::Async loop.

Impact: with auto_flush => 1 a Langfuse outage that black-holes or slow-drips (accepts TCP, never answers) delays EVERY chat iteration by up to 180s and freezes all other futures on the loop (knarr/skeid/raider style servers). The k278 timeout helper (_async_do_request_f / user_agent_timeout) is not used by either flush. A refused connection returns fast (LWP synthesizes 500 and flush only warns), so failure isolation for hard-down is fine; the slow case is not.

Also:
- Langfuse ingestion answers 207 Multi-Status with per-event errors; is_success is true for 207, so rejected events are silently lost.
- The whole batch goes in one POST with no size cap; Langfuse limits the ingestion body (a few MB). A large batch gets 413 and flush then clears it (all events lost).

Suggested fix: give both flushes a short timeout (own attribute, default ~5-10s), and in the async hooks send via the engine/host async transport (async_request_f with timeout) or defer with ->retain instead of blocking; inspect a 207 body and warn on errors; chunk the batch. Tests: local daemon that never answers (t/lib/Test/LocalHTTPDaemon) proving flush returns within the timeout and a chat_f with auto_flush is not delayed.
